.TH MESHWAV 1 "" "meshwav" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
meshwav \- private low-latency audio over your tailnet: send this computer's audio, or play audio sent from another device
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B meshwav
.br
.B meshwav send
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B meshwav receive
.RB { run | outputs | forget | "pair \\fICODE\\fR" }
.br
.B meshwav
.RB { pair | "pair \\fICODE\\fR" | devices | use " \\fIDEVICE\\fR|auto" | rename " \\fIDEVICE NEWNAME\\fR | remove " \\fIDEVICE\\fR }"
.SH DESCRIPTION
With no arguments it opens the window, which has a Receive / Send switch. Only one side runs at a time.
.PP
.B send
captures the system audio with parec, encodes it (Opus, or lossless PCM) and sends it, encrypted, over UDP to the paired receiver that is active. While a receiver is connected the default output is redirected to a virtual sink, so this computer plays nothing locally; it is handed back when the receiver leaves and when the sender exits. It only listens on a tailnet or loopback address.
Run it as a user service: \fBsystemctl --user enable --now meshwav-agent\fR (the window can set this up too).
.PP
.B receive
plays audio sent by another device through PulseAudio or PipeWire.
.SH COMMANDS
The sender's commands talk to the running sender over an owner-only socket (\fI~/.config/meshwav/control.sock\fR).
.TP
.B pair
Open a 2-minute pairing window and print a 6-digit code. Paired receivers on the tailnet are invited automatically; type the code on the receiver.
.TP
.BR "pair " \fICODE\fR
Pair this computer as a receiver with the code a sender is showing.
.TP
.B devices
List paired devices: online, active or standby.
.TP
.BR use " \\fIDEVICE\\fR|auto"
Send the audio to one device (name, name prefix or key prefix). \fBauto\fR lets the first connected device keep it.
.TP
.BR rename " \\fIDEVICE NEWNAME\\fR"
Rename a paired device.
.TP
.BR remove " \\fIDEVICE\\fR"
Forget a paired device. It is disconnected at once and must pair again with a new code.
.TP
.B receive run [\-\-device SINK] [\-\-lossless]
Play the paired sender's stream. \fBreceive outputs\fR lists the sound outputs.
.TP
.B receive forget
Forget the paired sender.
.SH SEND OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-bind " IP:PORT"
Address to listen on (default: tailscale IPv4, port 47900).
.TP
.BI \-\-codec " opus|lossless"
What is sent until a receiver asks for something else (default opus).
.TP
.BI \-\-kbps " N"
Opus bitrate, 64\-256 (default 160).
.TP
.BI \-\-frame\-ms " N"
Opus frame size: 5, 10 or 20 (default 10).
.TP
.BI \-\-source " SINK.monitor"
Capture from this source and do not redirect.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-redirect
Leave the local audio output alone.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " PATH"
Settings file (default \fI~/.config/meshwav/config.toml\fR).
.TP
.BI \-\-log\-level " LEVEL"
error, warn, info or debug.
.TP
.B \-\-pair
Open a pairing window at start-up.
.TP
.BI \-\-identity " PATH" ", " \-\-authorized " PATH"
Key and paired-device files.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/meshwav/config.toml
Flat key = value settings: bind, kbps, frame_ms, codec, source, no_redirect, log_level. See \fI/usr/share/doc/meshwav/config.toml.example\fR.
.TP
.I ~/.config/meshwav/{identity,authorized_peers,active_peer}
The sender's key, the paired devices and the chosen device.
.TP
.I ~/.config/meshwav/receiver/
The receiver's key, the paired sender, and its window settings.
.SH SEE ALSO
https://github.com/tech-house-blr/meshwav
